From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:44:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/i915/hdmi: HDR static metadata in the gamut metadata DIP
 of display version 9, as a choice

The HDMI port of display version 9 (Skylake, Broxton/Apollo Lake, Kaby
Lake, Coffee Lake, Comet Lake: HD and UHD 6xx) sends no HDR: its
transcoder has no DIP for the DRM InfoFrame, which display version 10
added (VIDEO_DIP_ENABLE_DRM_GLK), so i915 attaches HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA to
an HDMI connector from version 10 on, and to these only behind an
LSPCON, which sends the InfoFrame itself. A TV without Dolby Vision on
such a port never gets HDR from Hades: an HDR10 or HLG video is mapped
to SDR (with Dolby Vision, Hades sends HDR10 as Dolby Vision instead).
peachey777 saw HDR arrive as SDR on a UHD 630 over HDMI
(CroqueMr/libreelec-x86-DV issue 23).

Each transcoder of those versions has a gamut metadata DIP that nothing
uses over HDMI, and its header bytes, as those of the other DIPs, are
the first bytes written to it: over DisplayPort i915 sends the HDR
metadata InfoFrame SDP there from Haswell on (header 00 87 1d 4c,
intel_dp_hdr_metadata_infoframe_sdp_pack()), and Hades the Dolby vendor
InfoFrame SDP of a converter. CroqueMr's build sends the DRM InfoFrame
in it on Skylake and Kaby Lake (its linux-9900-07), "receiver
confirmation pending". Nothing here says that the DIP cannot carry it
over HDMI, and no display has been seen to take it from there; so it is
a choice, off by default, written here from i915 and not from that
patch.

i915.hdmi_hdr_gmp=1 (a display parameter, read-only, default 0): on an
HDMI port of display version 9 the DRM InfoFrame goes in the gamut
metadata DIP, with its own header (87 01 1a), and the port offers what
version 10 offers for HDR: HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA and HDR10PLUS_VSIF, the
HDR10 depth guard of Hades, and HDR over the Dolby Vision tunnel
(DVBRIDGE_HDR10_TUNNEL_LAB), which carries HDR10 at 12 bits as YCbCr
4:2:2 within the 8-bit RGB of 2160p24 that the port's 300 MHz allows.
The InfoFrame is crtc_state->infoframes.drm as everywhere; the bit of
infoframes.enable that sends it is that of the DIP it goes in, as over
DisplayPort, and the readout reads it from that DIP, only where the DIP
holds a DRM InfoFrame, so that the state check compares like with like.
The packing is the one of every other DIP (intel_hdmi_gmp_hdr.h), and
after each write the DIP is read back: what does not come back as
written is a kernel error. The vendor DIP stays what it was (the Dolby
VSIF of the tunnel, HDR10+, the HDMI VIC), and Kodi still turns
HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA off for Dolby Vision.

Without the parameter, and on every other display version, nothing
changes: the same decisions, DIPs and properties as before (the
decision is intel_hdmi_drm_dip_of(), tested on every version with the
parameter and without).

KUnit (drm_hdmi_gmp_hdr, 3 cases, in UML), with the kernel's InfoFrame
packers: where the InfoFrame goes on display versions 2 to 35 with the
parameter and without; the bytes the DIP is given for HDR10 metadata
(header 87 01 1a, the byte at 3, a checksum that sums the packet to 0,
the payload of hdmi_infoframe_pack_only(), 31 bytes within the 32 of
that DIP before version 11) and the same InfoFrame read back; and none
read from a DIP that holds a gamut metadata packet, an SDP, another
version or length of the InfoFrame, or a wrong checksum. Eleven mutants
of the header, each caught.

Not tried on a display. With the parameter, the kernel log says at load
"Hades: [CONNECTOR:..] HDR static metadata in the gamut metadata DIP of
display version 9 (i915.hdmi_hdr_gmp=1)", and for each HDR picture
"DRM (HDR static metadata) InfoFrame in the gamut metadata slot". A
display that does not take the packet from there shows HDR as a
washed-out picture: the parameter goes back to 0.
